.TH GPIO 3
.SH NAME
gpio \- general-purpose I/O pins as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #G /dev

.BI /dev/gpio/ N /ctl
.BI /dev/gpio/ N /level
.BI /dev/gpio/ N /event
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gpio
serves a directory for each pin of the Raspberry Pi's BCM2837,
.I N
being the pin's BCM number, 0 to 53, which is the only numbering the
hardware has.
A directory a pin, rather than one control file for all of them, so that
a namespace can give a program exactly one:
.IP
.EX
bind '#G/gpio/29' /mnt/led
.EE
.PP
The device is mechanism only: function, pull, level, edge.
Pin names, LED polarity, debouncing, counting and PWM are policy, and
belong to programs.
.PP
Pins the kernel drives itself \- the console's UART, the SD card, the
radio's \- are claimed by their drivers.
They can be read, and writes to their
.B ctl
fail with
.RI ` "in use by driver" '.
.SS ctl
Reading
.B ctl
gives the pin's configuration, a line each:
.IP
.EX
function out
pull none
edge none
.EE
.PP
.B Pull
reads as
.B unknown
until it has been set, because the hardware cannot be asked.
The messages that may be written are
.TP
.BI function " f"
where
.I f
is
.BR in ,
.BR out ,
or an alternate function
.B alt0
to
.BR alt5 .
.TP
.BI pull " p"
.BR up ,
.B down
or
.BR none .
.TP
.BI edge " e"
.BR rising ,
.BR falling ,
.B both
or
.BR none :
which transitions
.B event
reports.
.SS level
Reading
.B level
gives
.B 0
or
.B 1
and a newline.
Writing
.B 0
or
.B 1
drives a pin whose function is
.BR out .
.SS event
A read of
.B event
blocks until an edge of the kind
.B ctl
asked for has occurred, and then returns a line for each edge not yet
reported, oldest first:
.IP
.EX
1873226411 1
1873229042 0
.EE
.PP
The first field is the time of the interrupt in microseconds, on the clock
.IR bench (3)
serves; the second is the level after the edge.
Only the kernel can know that an edge happened and when, since only it
takes the interrupt: a program polling
.B level
learns late, cannot tell two edges from none, and never sleeps.
A reader that falls more than 256 edges behind loses the oldest, and the
next read begins with a line
.BI overrun " n"
saying how many.
Detection is enabled in the hardware only while an edge is asked for
.I and
an
.B event
file is open.
.SS "The firmware's expander"
Pins 128 to 135 are the eight lines of the GPIO expander the VideoCore
firmware drives, reached through the mailbox: on the 3B+ the radios'
power enables (128 Bluetooth, 129 Wi-Fi), the Ethernet chip's reset (131),
the power LED and HDMI hot-plug.
They have
.B ctl
and
.B level
and no
.BR event ,
the expander having no interrupt.
They have no function select and no pull, so
.B ctl
writes are refused;
.B ctl
reads report what the firmware says.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/bcm2837/devgpio.c
.br
.B /os/bcm2837/gpio.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bench (3)
.br
.B /tests/acceptance/gpio.py
(a loopback jig: eight pairs of header pins)
.SH BUGS
Raspberry Pi only; QEMU's
.B virt
machine has no pins, and its kernel omits the device.
.br
The desktop's namespace has
.B #G
removed from it; see
.IR osinit (8).
